./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/retry2_batch_075_097/full_2026-07-06__rec_003963__Phenotypic_Bioactivity_Prediction_as_Open-set_Biological_Assay_Queryin/metadata.json:6:  "abstract": "The traditional drug discovery pipeline is severely bottlenecked by the need to design and execute bespoke biological assays for every new target and compound—a process that is both time-consuming and prohibitively expensive. While machine learning has accelerated virtual screening, current models remain confined to “closed-set” paradigms, unable to generalize to entirely novel biological assays without target-specific experimental data. Here, we present\n                  OpenPheno\n                  , a groundbreaking multimodal foundation model that fundamentally redefines bioactivity prediction as an open-set, visual-language question-answering (QA) task. By integrating chemical structures (SMILES), universal phenotypic profiles (Cell Painting images), and natural language descriptions of biological assays, OpenPheno unlocks the highly coveted\n                  “profile once, predict many” paradigm\n                  . Instead of conducting countless target-specific wet-lab experiments, researchers only need to capture a single, low-cost Cell Painting image of a novel compound. OpenPheno then evaluates this universal phenotypic “fingerprint” against the text-based description of any unseen assay, predicting bioactivity in a zero-shot manner. On 54 entirely unseen assays, it achieves strong zero-shot performance (mean AUROC 0.75), exceeding supervised baselines trained with full labeled data, and few-shot adaptation further improves predictions. In the most stringent setting where both compounds and assays are novel, OpenPheno maintains robust generalization (mean AUROC 0.66), opening up a new paradigm for a highly scalable, cost-effective, and universal engine for next-generation drug discovery.",
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/retry_batch_120_149/full_2026-07-06__rec_003963__Phenotypic_Bioactivity_Prediction_as_Open-set_Biological_Assay_Queryin/metadata.json:6:  "abstract": "The traditional drug discovery pipeline is severely bottlenecked by the need to design and execute bespoke biological assays for every new target and compound—a process that is both time-consuming and prohibitively expensive. While machine learning has accelerated virtual screening, current models remain confined to “closed-set” paradigms, unable to generalize to entirely novel biological assays without target-specific experimental data. Here, we present\n                  OpenPheno\n                  , a groundbreaking multimodal foundation model that fundamentally redefines bioactivity prediction as an open-set, visual-language question-answering (QA) task. By integrating chemical structures (SMILES), universal phenotypic profiles (Cell Painting images), and natural language descriptions of biological assays, OpenPheno unlocks the highly coveted\n                  “profile once, predict many” paradigm\n                  . Instead of conducting countless target-specific wet-lab experiments, researchers only need to capture a single, low-cost Cell Painting image of a novel compound. OpenPheno then evaluates this universal phenotypic “fingerprint” against the text-based description of any unseen assay, predicting bioactivity in a zero-shot manner. On 54 entirely unseen assays, it achieves strong zero-shot performance (mean AUROC 0.75), exceeding supervised baselines trained with full labeled data, and few-shot adaptation further improves predictions. In the most stringent setting where both compounds and assays are novel, OpenPheno maintains robust generalization (mean AUROC 0.66), opening up a new paradigm for a highly scalable, cost-effective, and universal engine for next-generation drug discovery.",
./data/exports_update/springernature_raw_2026-04-14.json:1655:    "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) have recently been adopted to assist in the interpretation of human genomic variants. However, general-purpose LLMs can produce incorrect outputs (commonly termed ‘hallucinations’), particularly on specialized queries, raising concerns about their reliability for variant interpretation. Here, to mitigate this risk, we developed ChatTogoVar, a retrieval-augmented generation system that queries TogoVar, a variant database that integrates information, such as allele frequency and clinical significance, and incorporates the retrieved results into prompts. We constructed a benchmark of 150 questions sampled from a predefined pool of 1500 template–variant combinations (50 templates × 30 variants). For large-scale assessment, we used the full 1500-question pool for automated LLM-based scoring. ChatTogoVar achieved the highest score for 135/150 questions, outperforming both a general-purpose LLM and an existing specialized system. Furthermore, automatic evaluation of all 1500 questions by an LLM confirmed the same trend. These results suggest that integrating a reliable variant database with an LLM can improve the accuracy of variant interpretation and that ChatTogoVar may serve as a practical tool to support genomic medicine and personalized healthcare. As genomic medicine evolves, interpreting human variants becomes crucial, with variant databases such as TogoVar, gnomAD and ClinVar playing a key role. These databases help to determine variant frequency, disease association and interpretation reliability. However, accessing this information can be challenging for non-experts. The study introduces ChatTogoVar, a question-answering system that uses retrieval-augmented generation to reduce inaccuracies by integrating data from TogoVar, a comprehensive Japanese genomic variant database. ChatTogoVar was evaluated against general-purpose language models and VarChat, showing superior performance in accuracy and evidence support. It effectively integrates variant information, such as allele frequencies and clinical significance, from trusted databases. This approach minimizes errors and enhances genomic variant interpretation, crucial for personalized medicine. Future directions include expanding database integration to cover pharmacogenomics and complex queries, enhancing the system’s usability and reliability. This summary was initially drafted using artificial intelligence, then revised and fact-checked by the author.",
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/batch_000_049/full_2026-07-06__rec_000771__A_multimodal_conversational_agent_for_DNA_RNA_and_protein_tasks/metadata.json:6:  "abstract": "De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/candidates_250.json:668:      "abstract": "De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/candidates_250.json:2227:      "abstract": "Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/candidates_250.json:4560:      "abstract": "The traditional drug discovery pipeline is severely bottlenecked by the need to design and execute bespoke biological assays for every new target and compound—a process that is both time-consuming and prohibitively expensive. While machine learning has accelerated virtual screening, current models remain confined to “closed-set” paradigms, unable to generalize to entirely novel biological assays without target-specific experimental data. Here, we present\n                  OpenPheno\n                  , a groundbreaking multimodal foundation model that fundamentally redefines bioactivity prediction as an open-set, visual-language question-answering (QA) task. By integrating chemical structures (SMILES), universal phenotypic profiles (Cell Painting images), and natural language descriptions of biological assays, OpenPheno unlocks the highly coveted\n                  “profile once, predict many” paradigm\n                  . Instead of conducting countless target-specific wet-lab experiments, researchers only need to capture a single, low-cost Cell Painting image of a novel compound. OpenPheno then evaluates this universal phenotypic “fingerprint” against the text-based description of any unseen assay, predicting bioactivity in a zero-shot manner. On 54 entirely unseen assays, it achieves strong zero-shot performance (mean AUROC 0.75), exceeding supervised baselines trained with full labeled data, and few-shot adaptation further improves predictions. In the most stringent setting where both compounds and assays are novel, OpenPheno maintains robust generalization (mean AUROC 0.66), opening up a new paradigm for a highly scalable, cost-effective, and universal engine for next-generation drug discovery.",
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/candidates_250.json:4891:      "abstract": "Interpreting transcriptomic data is one of the most common analytical tasks in modern biology. Yet most current models either consume expression profiles without producing natural-language biological explanations, or reason in language without direct access to quantitative omics measurements. We introduce OmicsLM, a multimodal LLM that connects quantitative omics profiles with natural-language biological tasks. OmicsLM represents each transcriptomic profile as a compact continuous representation within the LLM context. This interface preserves quantitative expression signal while allowing natural-language instructions, explicit gene mentions, and multiple interleaved biological samples to be processed together in one model context. We train OmicsLM on more than 5.5 million instruction-following examples spanning over 70 task types, combining continuous transcriptomic inputs, experimental data rendered through diverse language templates, and free-text biological knowledge and question-answering data. This mixture covers cell type annotation, perturbation prediction, clinical prediction, pathway reasoning, and open-ended biological question answering. Existing benchmarks evaluate either profile-level prediction or text-only biological QA, leaving language-guided, multi-sample reasoning over real expression profiles unmeasured. To close this gap, we introduce GEO-OmicsQA, a benchmark for multi-sample biological question answering built from real Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) studies. We demonstrate that OmicsLM can use expression profiles directly and perform comparably to specialized omics models on profile-level tasks, while outperforming both omics-specialized models and general LLMs on language-guided biological reasoning over expression data.",
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:81:2191,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,EXCLUDE,,,"The abstract describes BioVERSE as a two-stage approach that aligns pretrained biomedical foundation models with LLMs via projection layers, enabling cross-modal reasoning and zero-shot annotation, which constitutes a primary model paper with substantive text-bio bridge. | The abstract describes a novel alignment and instruction-tuning system that leverages pretrained foundation models and produces generative outputs, but it does not explicitly characterize the system itself as a foundation model.","The abstract describes adapting pretrained BioFMs and LLMs via lightweight projection layers, which is a wrapper-style approach rather than introducing a new foundation model. Generative outputs are explicitly mentioned, confirming generative model presence."
./protocol/screening_prompt_regression_cases.csv:141:807,"A multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein tasks","De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,,,
./protocol/screening_prompt_templates/adjudicator_prompt.txt:68:- Chat/dialogue/question-answering about biological data, or single-cell analysis with natural language -> can support text component and text-bio bridge when central and primary.
./data/full_text_downloads_2026-07-07/retry4_batch_040_055/full_2026-07-06__rec_003963__Phenotypic_Bioactivity_Prediction_as_Open-set_Biological_Assay_Queryin/metadata.json:6:  "abstract": "The traditional drug discovery pipeline is severely bottlenecked by the need to design and execute bespoke biological assays for every new target and compound—a process that is both time-consuming and prohibitively expensive. While machine learning has accelerated virtual screening, current models remain confined to “closed-set” paradigms, unable to generalize to entirely novel biological assays without target-specific experimental data. Here, we present\n                  OpenPheno\n                  , a groundbreaking multimodal foundation model that fundamentally redefines bioactivity prediction as an open-set, visual-language question-answering (QA) task. By integrating chemical structures (SMILES), universal phenotypic profiles (Cell Painting images), and natural language descriptions of biological assays, OpenPheno unlocks the highly coveted\n                  “profile once, predict many” paradigm\n                  . Instead of conducting countless target-specific wet-lab experiments, researchers only need to capture a single, low-cost Cell Painting image of a novel compound. OpenPheno then evaluates this universal phenotypic “fingerprint” against the text-based description of any unseen assay, predicting bioactivity in a zero-shot manner. On 54 entirely unseen assays, it achieves strong zero-shot performance (mean AUROC 0.75), exceeding supervised baselines trained with full labeled data, and few-shot adaptation further improves predictions. In the most stringent setting where both compounds and assays are novel, OpenPheno maintains robust generalization (mean AUROC 0.66), opening up a new paradigm for a highly scalable, cost-effective, and universal engine for next-generation drug discovery.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:196:2191,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"biological text reasoning, LLMs, cross-modal question answering, interactive dialogue",aligns them with LLMs through projection layers; unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs,"enables richer, generative outputs; cross-modal question answering and interactive dialogue",clear_include,"Scope reviewer correctly identifies a primary model paper with explicit text-bio alignment and generative outputs; architecture reviewer's application_wrapper label is not supported because the abstract presents a new multimodal alignment and instruction-tuning framework that generates natural-language outputs, not a downstream classifier using frozen LLM embeddings.","Presents BioVERSE, a two-stage approach aligning biomedical modalities to LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling cross-modal reasoning and generative outputs. Clearly includes text component (LLM, instruction tuning, dialogue) and text-bio bridge (alignment of bio modalities to LLM space).","The abstract describes adapting pretrained BioFMs and aligning them with existing LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning, rather than presenting a new generative architecture; the generative outputs originate from the external LLM.","Scope reviewer correctly identifies a primary model paper with explicit text-bio alignment and generative outputs; architecture reviewer's application_wrapper label is not supported because the abstract presents a new multimodal alignment and instruction-tuning framework that generates natural-language outputs, not a downstream classifier using frozen LLM embeddings.",BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"LLMs, biological text reasoning, cross-modal question answering, interactive dialogue","aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers; unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs","enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs; cross-modal question answering, interactive, explainable dialogue",clear_include,"The abstract presents a new multi-modal alignment framework (BioVERSE) that adapts pretrained BioFMs and aligns them with LLMs for generative reasoning tasks, meeting all inclusion criteria; the architecture reviewer's wrapper classification is overruled because the approach is central and not merely using a frozen LLM for a downstream classifier.","The abstract presents BIOVERSE, a new model that aligns biomedical modalities with LLMs for cross-modal reasoning, including text-based question answering and dialogue, satisfying all criteria.","BioVERSE adapts pretrained BioFMs and aligns them with LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning; it does not propose a new generative architecture or foundation model, making it an application wrapper.","The abstract presents a new multi-modal alignment framework (BioVERSE) that adapts pretrained BioFMs and aligns them with LLMs for generative reasoning tasks, meeting all inclusion criteria; the architecture reviewer's wrapper classification is overruled because the approach is central and not merely using a frozen LLM for a downstream classifier.",BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,"cross-modal question answering, interactive explainable dialogue",unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs,"enables richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs",clear_include,"Abstract presents a new model (BIOVERSE) that aligns biomedical modalities with LLMs through projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling generative tasks like dialogue and question answering. This is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, and the generative and text-bridge criteria are clearly supported by the evidence.","BIOVERSE is a primary model that aligns biomedical modalities to LLMs via projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling cross-modal reasoning and generative outputs, satisfying all criteria.","The paper describes an alignment method that adapts pretrained BioFMs and LLMs, not a new generative architecture; it is an application wrapper around existing models.","Abstract presents a new model (BIOVERSE) that aligns biomedical modalities with LLMs through projection layers and instruction tuning, enabling generative tasks like dialogue and question answering. This is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, and the generative and text-bridge criteria are clearly supported by the evidence.",BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical data modalities with LLMs, includes natural‑language dialogue, and explicitly describes a bridge between modalities and language. | The abstract introduces BIOVERSE, a new two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained BioFMs with LLMs and applies instruction tuning, yielding generative outputs such as dialogue, which demonstrates a generative model; it also leverages large pretrained models, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.","The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical data modalities with LLMs, includes natural‑language dialogue, and explicitly describes a bridge between modalities and language.","The abstract introduces BIOVERSE, a new two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained BioFMs with LLMs and applies instruction tuning, yielding generative outputs such as dialogue, which demonstrates a generative model; it also leverages large pretrained models, indicating foundation‑model characteristics.",,BioVERSE: Representation Alignment of Biomedical Modalities to LLMs for Multi-Modal Reasoning,"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and biomedical foundation models (BioFMs) have achieved strong results in biological text reasoning, molecular modeling, and single-cell analysis, yet they remain siloed in disjoint embedding spaces, limiting cross-modal reasoning. We present BIOVERSE (Biomedical Vector Embedding Realignment for Semantic Engagement), a two-stage approach that adapts pretrained BioFMs as modality encoders and aligns them with LLMs through lightweight, modality-specific projection layers. The approach first aligns each modality to a shared LLM space through independently trained projections, allowing them to interoperate naturally, and then applies standard instruction tuning with multi-modal data to bring them together for downstream reasoning. By unifying raw biomedical data with knowledge embedded in LLMs, the approach enables zero-shot annotation, cross-modal question answering, and interactive, explainable dialogue. Across tasks spanning cell-type annotation, molecular description, and protein function reasoning, compact BIOVERSE configurations surpass larger LLM baselines while enabling richer, generative outputs than existing BioFMs, establishing a foundation for principled multi-modal biomedical reasoning.",include_decision_unstable,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical modalities to LLMs, providing biological data handling, natural‑language components, and a clear text‑bio bridge for reasoning. | The abstract presents BIOVERSE, a novel two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained biomedical encoders with LLMs and explicitly mentions ""enabling richer, generative outputs,"" indicating a generative foundation‑model contribution.","The abstract introduces BIOVERSE as a new multimodal model that aligns biomedical modalities to LLMs, providing biological data handling, natural‑language components, and a clear text‑bio bridge for reasoning.","The abstract presents BIOVERSE, a novel two‑stage architecture that aligns pretrained biomedical encoders with LLMs and explicitly mentions ""enabling richer, generative outputs,"" indicating a generative foundation‑model contribution.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:426:807,"A multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein tasks","De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",stable_include,"A multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein tasks","De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"ChatNT is presented as a new multimodal conversational agent that takes biological sequences and natural language instructions, achieving state-of-the-art results, satisfying all criteria. | ChatNT is introduced as a multimodal conversational agent that uses an English decoder to generate responses, providing clear generative evidence. The abstract also describes transfer learning and state-of-the-art results on a benchmark, indicating foundation-model characteristics.","ChatNT is presented as a new multimodal conversational agent that takes biological sequences and natural language instructions, achieving state-of-the-art results, satisfying all criteria.","ChatNT is introduced as a multimodal conversational agent that uses an English decoder to generate responses, providing clear generative evidence. The abstract also describes transfer learning and state-of-the-art results on a benchmark, indicating foundation-model characteristics.",,"A multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein tasks","De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes ChatNT as a multimodal conversational agent that takes natural language instructions and produces English outputs based on DNA, RNA, and protein sequences, clearly satisfying all criteria for a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | The abstract introduces ChatNT as a conversational agent generating natural language responses, using a decoder architecture, and leverages transfer learning from foundation models, satisfying generative model and foundation model evidence.","The abstract describes ChatNT as a multimodal conversational agent that takes natural language instructions and produces English outputs based on DNA, RNA, and protein sequences, clearly satisfying all criteria for a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract introduces ChatNT as a conversational agent generating natural language responses, using a decoder architecture, and leverages transfer learning from foundation models, satisfying generative model and foundation model evidence.",,"A multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein tasks","De Almeida, Richard and colleagues leverage transfer learning to create ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent for DNA, RNA and protein sequences that can be instructed in natural language. Language models are thriving, powering conversational agents that assist and empower humans to solve a number of tasks. Recently, these models were extended to support additional modalities including vision, audio and video, demonstrating impressive capabilities across multiple domains, including healthcare. Still, conversational agents remain limited in biology as they cannot yet fully comprehend biological sequences. Meanwhile, high-performance foundation models for biological sequences have been built through self-supervision over sequencing data, but these need to be fine-tuned for each specific application, preventing generalization between tasks. In addition, these models are not conversational, which limits their utility to users with coding capabilities. Here we propose to bridge the gap between biology foundation models and conversational agents by introducing ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent with an advanced understanding of biological sequences. ChatNT achieves new state-of-the-art results on the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark while being able to solve all tasks at once, in English, and to generalize to unseen questions. In addition, we have curated a set of more biologically relevant instruction tasks from DNA, RNA and proteins, spanning multiple species, tissues and biological processes. ChatNT reaches performance on par with state-of-the-art specialized methods on those tasks. We also present a perplexity-based technique to help calibrate the confidence of our model predictions. By applying attribution methods through the English decoder and DNA encoder, we demonstrate that ChatNT’s answers are based on biologically coherent features such as detecting the promoter TATA motif or splice site dinucleotides. Our framework for genomics instruction tuning can be extended to more tasks and data modalities (for example, structure and imaging), making it a widely applicable tool for biology. ChatNT provides a potential direction for building generally capable agents that understand biology from first principles while being accessible to users with no coding background.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"Presents ChatNT, a multimodal conversational agent that uses natural language instructions to perform DNA, RNA, and protein tasks, clearly meeting all criteria. | The abstract describes