tain.","Title and abstract describe conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation with large language models and emphasize pretraining on extensive DNA text corpora, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model characteristics.","Title and abstract indicate a new multimodal model that uses instruction (natural-language) tuning to condition DNA sequence generation with a large language model, satisfying all inclusion criteria.",Multimodal Cell Context Instruction Tuning for Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation with Large Language Models,-trained Geneformer single cell transcriptomes encoder [ -GPT-2. These results emphasize  the critical role of pretraining on extensive DNA text corpora in enhancing both the generative,stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The title describes a new multimodal model that uses instruction tuning (natural‑language) to condition DNA sequence generation, linking cell‑context biology with text prompts. | The title and abstract explicitly mention conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation using large language models and pretraining on extensive DNA text corpora, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model characteristics.","The title describes a new multimodal model that uses instruction tuning (natural‑language) to condition DNA sequence generation, linking cell‑context biology with text prompts.","The title and abstract explicitly mention conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation using large language models and pretraining on extensive DNA text corpora, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model characteristics.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:686:85,ProCyon: A multimodal foundation model for protein phenotypes.,"Characterizing human proteins remains a major challenge: approximately 29% of human proteins lack experimentally validated functions and even well-annotated proteins often lack context-specific phenotypic insights. To enable universal modeling of protein phenotypes, we present P  ro C  yon , a multimodal foundation model that utilizes protein sequence, structure, and natural language for generating and predicting protein phenotypes across diverse knowledge domains. P  ro C  yon is trained on our novel dataset, P  ro C  yon -I  nstruct , with 33 million protein phenotype instructions. On dozens of benchmarking tasks, P  ro C  yon performs competitively against single-modal and multimodal models. Further, P  ro C  yon conditionally retrieves proteins via mechanisms of action of small molecule drugs and disease contexts, and it generates candidate phenotypic descriptions for poorly characterized proteins, including those implicated in Parkinson’s disease that were identified after P  ro C  yon ’s knowledge cutoff date. We experimentally confirm P  ro C  yon ’s predictions in multiple sclerosis using post-mortem brain RNA-seq, identifying novel MS genes and elucidating associated pathway mechanisms consistent with cortical pathology. P  ro C  yon paves the way toward a general approach to generate functional insights into the human proteome.",stable_include,ProCyon: A multimodal foundation model for protein phenotypes.,"Characterizing human proteins remains a major challenge: approximately 29% of human proteins lack experimentally validated functions and even well-annotated proteins often lack context-specific phenotypic insights. To enable universal modeling of protein phenotypes, we present P  ro C  yon , a multimodal foundation model that utilizes protein sequence, structure, and natural language for generating and predicting protein phenotypes across diverse knowledge domains. P  ro C  yon is trained on our novel dataset, P  ro C  yon -I  nstruct , with 33 million protein phenotype instructions. On dozens of benchmarking tasks, P  ro C  yon performs competitively against single-modal and multimodal models. Further, P  ro C  yon conditionally retrieves proteins via mechanisms of action of small molecule drugs and disease contexts, and it generates candidate phenotypic descriptions for poorly characterized proteins, including those implicated in Parkinson’s disease that were identified after P  ro C  yon ’s knowledge cutoff date. We experimentally confirm P  ro C  yon ’s predictions in multiple sclerosis using post-mortem brain RNA-seq, identifying novel MS genes and elucidating associated pathway mechanisms consistent with cortical pathology. P  ro C  yon paves the way toward a general approach to generate functional insights into the human proteome.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that integrates protein sequence, structure, and natural language to generate and predict protein phenotypes, clearly meeting all criteria for a primary text-bio generative model. | Abstract describes a multimodal foundation model that generates protein phenotype descriptions, with broad pretraining on 33M instructions and competitive performance on dozens of tasks, clearly indicating a generative foundation model.","The abstract describes ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that integrates protein sequence, structure, and natural language to generate and predict protein phenotypes, clearly meeting all criteria for a primary text-bio generative model.","Abstract describes a multimodal foundation model that generates protein phenotype descriptions, with broad pretraining on 33M instructions and competitive performance on dozens of tasks, clearly indicating a generative foundation model.",,ProCyon: A multimodal foundation model for protein phenotypes.,"Characterizing human proteins remains a major challenge: approximately 29% of human proteins lack experimentally validated functions and even well-annotated proteins often lack context-specific phenotypic insights. To enable universal modeling of protein phenotypes, we present P  ro C  yon , a multimodal foundation model that utilizes protein sequence, structure, and natural language for generating and predicting protein phenotypes across diverse knowledge domains. P  ro C  yon is trained on our novel dataset, P  ro C  yon -I  nstruct , with 33 million protein phenotype instructions. On dozens of benchmarking tasks, P  ro C  yon performs competitively against single-modal and multimodal models. Further, P  ro C  yon conditionally retrieves proteins via mechanisms of action of small molecule drugs and disease contexts, and it generates candidate phenotypic descriptions for poorly characterized proteins, including those implicated in Parkinson’s disease that were identified after P  ro C  yon ’s knowledge cutoff date. We experimentally confirm P  ro C  yon ’s predictions in multiple sclerosis using post-mortem brain RNA-seq, identifying novel MS genes and elucidating associated pathway mechanisms consistent with cortical pathology. P  ro C  yon paves the way toward a general approach to generate functional insights into the human proteome.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that explicitly uses natural language alongside protein sequence/structure to generate phenotypic descriptions, satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | The abstract presents a new multimodal foundation model that generates protein phenotype descriptions using protein sequence, structure, and natural language, meeting generative and foundation model criteria.","The abstract presents ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that explicitly uses natural language alongside protein sequence/structure to generate phenotypic descriptions, satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","The abstract presents a new multimodal foundation model that generates protein phenotype descriptions using protein sequence, structure, and natural language, meeting generative and foundation model criteria.",,ProCyon: A multimodal foundation model for protein phenotypes.,"Characterizing human proteins remains a major challenge: approximately 29% of human proteins lack experimentally validated functions and even well-annotated proteins often lack context-specific phenotypic insights. To enable universal modeling of protein phenotypes, we present P  ro C  yon , a multimodal foundation model that utilizes protein sequence, structure, and natural language for generating and predicting protein phenotypes across diverse knowledge domains. P  ro C  yon is trained on our novel dataset, P  ro C  yon -I  nstruct , with 33 million protein phenotype instructions. On dozens of benchmarking tasks, P  ro C  yon performs competitively against single-modal and multimodal models. Further, P  ro C  yon conditionally retrieves proteins via mechanisms of action of small molecule drugs and disease contexts, and it generates candidate phenotypic descriptions for poorly characterized proteins, including those implicated in Parkinson’s disease that were identified after P  ro C  yon ’s knowledge cutoff date. We experimentally confirm P  ro C  yon ’s predictions in multiple sclerosis using post-mortem brain RNA-seq, identifying novel MS genes and elucidating associated pathway mechanisms consistent with cortical pathology. P  ro C  yon paves the way toward a general approach to generate functional insights into the human proteome.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"ProCyon is a new multimodal foundation model integrating protein sequence, structure, and natural language to generate and predict protein phenotypes, with explicit text generation and instruction tuning, clearly meeting all inclusion criteria. | ProCyon is explicitly described as a multimodal foundation model that generates protein phenotype descriptions, meeting criteria for generative and foundation model evidence.","ProCyon is a new multimodal foundation model integrating protein sequence, structure, and natural language to generate and predict protein phenotypes, with explicit text generation and instruction tuning, clearly meeting all inclusion criteria.","ProCyon is explicitly described as a multimodal foundation model that generates protein phenotype descriptions, meeting criteria for generative and foundation model evidence.",,ProCyon: A multimodal foundation model for protein phenotypes.,"Characterizing human proteins remains a major challenge: approximately 29% of human proteins lack experimentally validated functions and even well-annotated proteins often lack context-specific phenotypic insights. To enable universal modeling of protein phenotypes, we present P  ro C  yon , a multimodal foundation model that utilizes protein sequence, structure, and natural language for generating and predicting protein phenotypes across diverse knowledge domains. P  ro C  yon is trained on our novel dataset, P  ro C  yon -I  nstruct , with 33 million protein phenotype instructions. On dozens of benchmarking tasks, P  ro C  yon performs competitively against single-modal and multimodal models. Further, P  ro C  yon conditionally retrieves proteins via mechanisms of action of small molecule drugs and disease contexts, and it generates candidate phenotypic descriptions for poorly characterized proteins, including those implicated in Parkinson’s disease that were identified after P  ro C  yon ’s knowledge cutoff date. We experimentally confirm P  ro C  yon ’s predictions in multiple sclerosis using post-mortem brain RNA-seq, identifying novel MS genes and elucidating associated pathway mechanisms consistent with cortical pathology. P  ro C  yon paves the way toward a general approach to generate functional insights into the human proteome.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces ProCyon as a new multimodal foundation model that combines protein data with natural language to generate phenotype descriptions, satisfying criteria for a primary model, biological modality, text component, and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that ""generates candidate phenotypic descriptions"", indicating a generative architecture, and notes broad pretraining on 33 M protein phenotype instructions, satisfying foundation‑model criteria.","The abstract introduces ProCyon as a new multimodal foundation model that combines protein data with natural language to generate phenotype descriptions, satisfying criteria for a primary model, biological modality, text component, and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that ""generates candidate phenotypic descriptions"", indicating a generative architecture, and notes broad pretraining on 33 M protein phenotype instructions, satisfying foundation‑model criteria.",,ProCyon: A multimodal foundation model for protein phenotypes.,"Characterizing human proteins remains a major challenge: approximately 29% of human proteins lack experimentally validated functions and even well-annotated proteins often lack context-specific phenotypic insights. To enable universal modeling of protein phenotypes, we present P  ro C  yon , a multimodal foundation model that utilizes protein sequence, structure, and natural language for generating and predicting protein phenotypes across diverse knowledge domains. P  ro C  yon is trained on our novel dataset, P  ro C  yon -I  nstruct , with 33 million protein phenotype instructions. On dozens of benchmarking tasks, P  ro C  yon performs competitively against single-modal and multimodal models. Further, P  ro C  yon conditionally retrieves proteins via mechanisms of action of small molecule drugs and disease contexts, and it generates candidate phenotypic descriptions for poorly characterized proteins, including those implicated in Parkinson’s disease that were identified after P  ro C  yon ’s knowledge cutoff date. We experimentally confirm P  ro C  yon ’s predictions in multiple sclerosis using post-mortem brain RNA-seq, identifying novel MS genes and elucidating associated pathway mechanisms consistent with cortical pathology. P  ro C  yon paves the way toward a general approach to generate functional insights into the human proteome.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents ProCyon as a new multimodal foundation model that combines protein sequence/structure (biological modality) with natural‑language inputs and generates phenotypic descriptions, satisfying both text component and text‑bio bridge criteria. | The abstract presents ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that ""generates candidate phenotypic descriptions"" and is trained on 33 million protein phenotype instructions, indicating both generative capability and broad foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract presents ProCyon as a new multimodal foundation model that combines protein sequence/structure (biological modality) with natural‑language inputs and generates phenotypic descriptions, satisfying both text component and text‑bio bridge criteria.","The abstract presents ProCyon as a multimodal foundation model that ""generates candidate phenotypic descriptions"" and is trained on 33 million protein phenotype instructions, indicating both generative capability and broad foundation-model characteristics.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/manuscript_appendix_c_prompts.md:212:- Chat/dialogue/question-answering about biological data, or single-cell analysis with natural language -> can support text component and text-bio bridge when central and primary.
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/unstable_reasoning_cases.csv:38:3359,Multimodal Cell Context Instruction Tuning for Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation with Large Language Models,stable_include,UNCERTAIN|UNCERTAIN|UNCERTAIN|INCLUDE|INCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,thin_abstract_boundary,none,none,Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation,"Abstract is truncated; title suggests a generative model for DNA sequences with multimodal cell context and instruction tuning, but no explicit natural-language text component or text-bio bridge is confirmed. Text component and bridge remain unclear due to insufficient abstract evidence.",UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,thin_abstract_boundary,"Instruction Tuning (title) but abstract truncated, no explicit natural-language text",Multimodal Cell Context and Conditional DNA Generation (title) but bridge unclear due to text component uncertainty,Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation (title) and 'generative' in abstract,"Title presents a multimodal instruction-tuning framework for conditional DNA generation using LLMs, but the truncated abstract lacks explicit evidence of a natural-language text component and text-bio bridge. Text component is unclear.",UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,thin_abstract_boundary,none,none,Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation,"Title explicitly presents a primary model for conditional DNA sequence generation using LLMs and instruction tuning, but the truncated abstract lacks explicit evidence of natural-language text or text-bio alignment, leaving text component and bridge unclear.",INCLUDE,,,clear_include,Instruction Tuning,Conditional DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation,DNA Regulatory Sequence Generation,"Title and abstract indicate a new multimodal model that uses instruction (natural-language) tuning to condition DNA sequence generation with a large language model, satisfying all inclusion criteria.",INCLUDE,,,,,,,"The title describes a new multimodal model that uses instruction tuning (natural‑language) to condition DNA sequence generation, linking cell‑context biology with text prompts. | The title and abstract explicitly mention conditional DNA regulatory sequence generation using large language models and pretraining on extensive DNA text corpora, indicating a generative architecture with foundation-model characteristics.",thin_or_truncated_abstract;text_component_boundary;generative_boundary
