ract mentions an instruction-tuning strategy that aligns gene expression data with another modality. That supports a substantive text-bio bridge within the model.","evidence_snippet":"“ZerOmics: Toward General Models for Single-Cell Analysis with Instruction Tuning”; “aligns SC gene expression data”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003118","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title and abstract point to a new general model with instruction tuning, which is foundation-model-like. However, the fragment does not explicitly describe a generative decoder or autoregressive mechanism.","evidence_snippet":"\"Toward General Models for Single-Cell Analysis with Instruction Tuning\"; \"innovative dual-alignment strategy\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003119","title":"A Transformer-Based Foundation Model for Human Microbiome Analysis","abstract":"gave rise to LLaMA (Large Language Model Meta AI) [12], which  that this loss of gene  expression data can cause the rank- single-cell data is sparse, small changes in gene expression","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"NA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003119","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title is about a transformer-based foundation model for human microbiome analysis, but the abstract fragment only supports microbiome/gene-expression style biology and does not clearly show a text or language component in the candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"“A Transformer-Based Foundation Model for Human Microbiome Analysis”; “single-cell data is sparse”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003119","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly claims a transformer-based foundation model for human microbiome analysis, and the abstract fragment mentions pretraining on large data. The generative architecture itself is not clearly described in the provided text.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Transformer-Based Foundation Model for Human Microbiome Analysis\"; \"By pretraining on large single-cell or bulk RNA\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003120","title":"Foundation Model in Biomedicine","abstract":"new paradigm for modeling gene expression data. By pretraining on large single-cell or bulk  RNA  Psychbert: a mental health language model for social media mental health behavioral","doi":"","year":"2025","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003120","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title is generic and the abstract reads like a survey of foundation models in biomedicine, citing examples such as Psychbert. This is not presented as a primary candidate model paper.","evidence_snippet":"“Foundation Model in Biomedicine”; “new paradigm for modeling gene expression data”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003120","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a broad review-style piece on foundation models in biomedicine rather than a primary model paper. The abstract fragment discusses pretraining as a paradigm but does not identify a specific candidate model with generative details.","evidence_snippet":"\"Foundation Model in Biomedicine\"; \"new paradigm for modeling gene expression data\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_003123","title":"Representation learning of single-cell RNA-seq data","abstract":"Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) has become a cornerstone experimental technique in tissue biology, with gene expression data for over 100 million cells available in public repositories. The high dimensionality, sparsity, and technical noise inherent to scRNA-seq data have motivated the development of a broad spectrum of representation learning approaches. These methods learn compressed, lower-dimensional representations of single-cell transcriptomes that are meant to preserve essential variation while reducing noise, and can be used for clustering, visualization, trajectory inference, and other downstream tasks. Furthermore, methods have emerged that aim to integrate data from multiple experiments by learning a common latent representation. In this review, we frame factor models, autoencoders, contrastive learning approaches, and transformer-based foundation models as distinct instances of the representation learning paradigm for scRNA-seq. We provide a coherent taxonomy of these methods that articulates their conceptual foundations, shared assumptions, and key distinctions. We also discuss benchmarking and identify major challenges and open questions that will shape the future of the field.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"RNA","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003123","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says, \"In this review,\" and it frames existing scRNA-seq methods rather than presenting a new model. It discusses transformer-based foundation models as a survey topic.","evidence_snippet":"\"In this review, we frame ... transformer-based foundation models\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_003123","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review that provides a taxonomy of representation learning methods. It does not present a new model or foundation-model pretraining evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"We provide a coherent taxonomy\"; \"This is a review\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
