ing the potency of anesthetics. In this study, we introduce a proteomic learning of GABA receptor-mediated anesthesia based on 24 GABA receptor subtypes by considering over 4000 proteins in protein–protein interaction (PPI) networks and over 1.5 millions known binding compounds. We develop a corresponding drug–target interaction network to identify potential lead compounds for novel anesthetic design. To ensure robust proteomic learning predictions, we curated a data set comprising 136 targets from a pool of 980 targets within the PPI networks. We employed three machine learning algorithms, integrating advanced natural language processing (NLP) models such as pretrained transformers and autoencoder embeddings. Through a comprehensive screening process, we evaluated the side effects and repurposing potential of over 180,000 drug candidates targeting the GABRA5 receptor. Additionally, we assessed the ADMET (absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion, and toxicity) properties of these candidates to identify those with near-optimal characteristics. This approach also involved optimizing the structures of existing anesthetics. Our work presents an innovative strategy for the development of new anesthetic drugs, optimization of anesthetic use, and a deeper understanding of potential anesthesia-related side effects.","doi":"10.1021/acs.jcim.5c00114","year":"2025","venue":"Journal of Chemical Information and Modeling","sources":["scopus","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000773","title":"The impact of artificial intelligence on precision medicine and personalized oncology: A systematic review with narrative synthesis","abstract":"&lt;b&gt;Background&lt;/b&gt;: The use of artificial intelligence (AI) in cancer treatment attempts to improve precision and customization. This integration could enhance treatment outcomes, reduce side effects, and optimize healthcare resource allocation as cancer continues to climb globally.&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;b&gt;Aims&lt;/b&gt;: This study examines how AI advances personalized oncology by predicting treatment responses, improving outcomes, and addressing ethical and privacy challenges.&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;b&gt;Methods&lt;/b&gt;: The study conducted a systematic review of AI applications in personalized oncology, synthesizing research on machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) in diagnostics, prognostics, and treatment personalization. It reviewed AI’s role in analyzing multi-omics, clinical, and imaging data for cancer therapy selection. Primary data analysis using Smart PLS software further assessed AI’s effectiveness in treatment recommendations, emphasizing the need for data standardization and validation for clinical integration.&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;b&gt;Results&lt;/b&gt;: This review found that predictive modeling with biomarkers, multi-omics, and histopathology data enables AI to analyze complex cancer datasets, enhancing diagnostic and treatment outcomes. DL and ML contribute to personalized oncology by predicting patient responses and identifying treatment targets. However, challenges such as data standardization, algorithm transparency, and ethical considerations need to be addressed to ensure the responsible use of AI in this field.&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;b&gt;Conclusion&lt;/b&gt;: The potential of AI to enhance the precision of cancer treatment and personalize patient care while acknowledging challenges such as data transparency, ethical sharing, and collaboration is highly likely. Ongoing research and integrating various ML methods are crucial for successfully implementing these advancements in clinical practice.","doi":"10.29333/ejgm/17046","year":"2025","venue":"Electronic Journal of General Medicine","sources":["scopus"]},{"record_id":"rec_000774","title":"Envisioning the Future of Machine Learning in the Early Detection of Neurodevelopmental and Neurodegenerative Disorders via Speech and Language Biomarkers","abstract":"Speech and language offer a rich, non-invasive window into brain health. Advances in machine learning (ML) have enabled increasingly accurate detection of neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders through these modalities. This paper envisions the future of ML in the early detection of neurodevelopmental disorders like autism spectrum disorder and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, and neurodegenerative disorders, such as Parkinson’s disease and Alzheimer’s disease, through speech and language biomarkers. We explore the current landscape of ML techniques, including deep learning and multimodal approaches, and review their applications across various conditions, highlighting both successes and inherent limitations. Our core contribution lies in outlining future trends across several critical dimensions. These include the enhancement of data availability and quality, the evolution of models, the development of multilingual and cross-cultural models, the establishment of regulatory and clinical translation frameworks, and the creation of hybrid systems enabling human–artificial intelligence (AI) collaboration. Finally, we conclude with a vision for future directions, emphasizing the potential integration of ML-driven speech diagnostics into public health infrastructure, the development of patient-specific explainable AI, and its synergistic combination with genomics and brain imaging for holistic brain health assessment. Overcoming substantial hurdles in validation, generalization, and clinical adoption, the field is poised to shift toward ubiquitous, accessible, and highly personalized tools for early diagnosis.","doi":"10.3390/acoustics7040072","year":"2025","venue":"Acoustics","sources":["scopus","semantic_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000775","title":"Foundation model of neural activity predicts response to new stimulus types","abstract":"A foundation model trained on neural activity of visual cortex from multiple mice accurately predicts responses to video stimuli and cell types, dendritic features and connectivity within the MICrONS functional connectomics dataset. The complexity of neural circuits makes it challenging to decipher the brain’s algorithms of intelligence. Recent breakthroughs in deep learning have produced models that accurately simulate brain activity, enhancing our understanding of the brain’s computational objectives and neural coding. However, it is difficult for such models to generalize beyond their training distribution, limiting their utility. The emergence of foundation models^ 1 trained on vast datasets has introduced a new artificial intelligence paradigm with remarkable generalization capabilities. Here we collected large amounts of neural activity from visual cortices of multiple mice and trained a foundation model to accurately predict neuronal responses to arbitrary natural videos. This model generalized to new mice with minimal training and successfully predicted responses across various new stimulus domains, such as coherent motion and noise patterns. Beyond neural response prediction, the model also accurately predicted anatomical cell types, dendritic features and neuronal connectivity within the MICrONS functional connectomics dataset^ 2 . Our work is a crucial step towards building foundation models of the brain. As neuroscience accumulates larger, multimodal datasets, foundation models will reveal statistical regularities, enable rapid adaptation to new tasks and accelerate research.","doi":"10.1038/s41586-025-08829-y","year":"2025","venue":"Nature","sources":["scopus","springernature"]},{"record_id":"rec_000776","title":"Swin Transformer and Momentum Contrast (MoCo) in Leukemia Diagnostics: A New Paradigm in AI-Driven Blood Cell Cancer Classification","abstract":"Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that requires prompt diagnosis for effective treatment. Automated image diagnostics offer potential solutions but often lack clinical robustness. Despite their widespread use in medical imaging, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) struggle to differentiate morphologically similar ALL subtypes due to limited context and feature discrimination. Moreover, integrating contrastive self-supervised learning with hierarchical attention-based models remains underexplored in hematologic malignancy classification. This study aims to develop a robust, automated classification model for ALL subtypes using peripheral blood smear images, employing advanced feature extraction through the Swin Transformer framework, combined with Momentum Contrast (MoCo) for contrastive learning and a Bidirectional Encoder Transformer for classification. The Swin Transformer’s patch-based embedding and multi-level attention enhance feature discrimination across ALL subtypes, while MoCo generates distinct embeddings, minimizing overlap between cell types. BiET is employed to classify the refined feature vectors, leveraging self-attention mechanisms to improve classification accuracy. The model achieved an overall classification accuracy of 92.5%, with the precision of 90.3%, a recall of 91.1%, and an F1-score of 90.7% across four classes (Benign, Malignant Early Pre-B, Malignant Pre-B, and Malignant Pro-B). Class-specific performance metrics indicate that Malignant Pre-B achieved the highest F1-score of 92.4%. The MoCo framework reduced contrastive loss from 0.5 to 0.097 for benign cells, enhancing feature discrimination. An ablation study revealed that omitting the dynamic queue decreased accuracy by 5%, underscoring its importance for effective feature learning. This approach can be extended to other hematologic malignancies, with potential for further improvement using larger datasets and real-time diagnostic workflows to support precision medicine.","doi":"10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3563256","year":"2025","venue":"IEEE Access","sources":["scopus"]}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0320.stderr.log:607:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0244.stderr.log:1588:./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0096.stderr.log:112:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000761","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a review of AI applications in natural product science, not a primary model paper. It discusses generative AI and knowledge graphs, but the abstract does not define a specific text-bio model bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review demonstrates the latest developments in AI applications for NP drug discovery and genomics\"; mentions \"generative AI\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000762","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper evaluates GPT-3.5/GPT-4 as a curation and question-answering tool over genetics and genomic articles. This is an external LLM wrapper for a bioinformatics task, not a new text-bio foundational model.","evidence_snippet":"\"GPT-based retrieval-augmented generation question-answering system\" for wheat and barley genetics data."},{"record_id":"rec_000763","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a new PEDL+ pipeline that uses deep language models for protein-centered relation extraction and then validates the findings in vivo. The text and biological components are coupled in the method itself.","evidence_snippet":"\"protein-centered relation extraction using deep language models\" together with \"in vivo experimental validation\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000764","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChatGPT with GPT-4o is only used to generate and rank hypotheses for cardiotoxicity research. That is an application of an existing LLM, not a substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"ChatGPT with GPT-4o was used to generate multiple hypotheses\" and experimental plans."},{"record_id":"rec_000765","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The core contribution is a cancer functional proteomics resource, with an LLM-powered chatbot layered on top. The abstract centers the resource and platform rather than a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"comprehensive cancer functional proteomics resource\" and \"DrBioRight 2.0, an intuitive bioinformatic platform powered by... large language models\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000766","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a clinically oriented review of computer-aided diagnosis systems for lung cancer. The abstract focuses on imaging and ML/DL systems, not a text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This clinically oriented review synthesizes computer-aided diagnosis across classical imaging, machine learning, and deep learning\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000767","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says it systematically reviews representative LLMs and their biological applications across genome, transcriptome, and proteome fields. It is a review, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"we first systematically review representative LLMs in the biological domain\" and their \"applications across the genome, transcriptome, and proteome fields\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000768","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a new protein language model-driven framework trained on curated GR sequences and applied at genome scale. The text-sequence model is directly tied to the biological discovery task.","evidence_snippet":"\"deep protein language models\" and a \"deep learning-based GR identification model trained on 74,872 curated GR sequences\"."}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0460.stderr.log:1180:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0053.response.txt:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_000417","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"unclear","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title is a healthcare responsibility review, and the abstract reads like a fragment of examples rather than one primary model paper. No single text-bio foundational model is clearly described.","evidence_snippet":"\"Responsible Foundation Models for Healthcare: Risks, Opportunities, and Pathways to Responsibility\"; \"multi-omics data ... Mammo-CLIP\""},{"record_id":"rec_000418","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes an autonomous AI system that constructs perturbation-response models using an AI coding agent and multimodal biological foundation models. That is a wrapper/system around existing models, not a new text-bio foundational model.","evidence_snippet":"\"combining an AI coding agent with multimodal biological foundation models\""},{"record_id":"rec_000419","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"no","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a visual anomaly detection application that evaluates a pre-trained multimodal model and uses LLM capabilities for rephrasing. It is not a biological text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"pre-trained multimodal foundation model\"; \"LLM capabilities such as rephrasing\""},{"record_id":"rec_000420","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title indicates a computational pathology foundation model for whole-slide image analysis via vector quantization. The abstract supports a biomedical image model, but no text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"computational pathology foundation model for whole slide image analysis via vector quantization\""},{"record_id":"rec_000421","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"unclear","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly says comprehensive review. The abstract mentions example models and modalities, but does not present a single primary text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"The Role of Artificial Intelligence in Modern Biotechnology: A Comprehensive Review\"; \"AlphaFold 3 ... single-cell RNA sequencing ... Virchow model\""},{"record_id":"rec_000422","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a hardware port and benchmark of an existing Geneformer model, not a new candidate model. The abstract supports single-cell transcriptomics, but not a clearly described text-language bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"porting, optimization, and benchmarking of the Geneformer model\"; \"single-cell transcriptomics\""},{"record_id":"rec_000423","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper proposes pathway-structured privileged distillation for computational pathology using WSI patch features and genomic/transcriptomic measurements. The abstract does not indicate a text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"genomic or transcriptomic measurements\"; \"WSI patch features extracted by a frozen pathology foundation model\""},{"record_id":"rec_000424","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract points to visual instruction tuning on a medical vision-language foundation model and mentions histology-genomic analysis. It still reads as an application or adaptation of an existing multimodal model rather than a primary text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"visual instruction tuning\"; \"vision language foundation model\"; \"histologygenomic analysis via multimodal\""}]}
