_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"ChatGPT with GPT-4o is only used to generate and rank hypotheses for cardiotoxicity research. That is an application of an existing LLM, not a substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"ChatGPT with GPT-4o was used to generate multiple hypotheses\" and experimental plans."},{"record_id":"rec_000765","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The core contribution is a cancer functional proteomics resource, with an LLM-powered chatbot layered on top. The abstract centers the resource and platform rather than a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"comprehensive cancer functional proteomics resource\" and \"DrBioRight 2.0, an intuitive bioinformatic platform powered by... large language models\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000766","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a clinically oriented review of computer-aided diagnosis systems for lung cancer. The abstract focuses on imaging and ML/DL systems, not a text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This clinically oriented review synthesizes computer-aided diagnosis across classical imaging, machine learning, and deep learning\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000767","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says it systematically reviews representative LLMs and their biological applications across genome, transcriptome, and proteome fields. It is a review, not a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"we first systematically review representative LLMs in the biological domain\" and their \"applications across the genome, transcriptome, and proteome fields\"."},{"record_id":"rec_000768","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a new protein language model-driven framework trained on curated GR sequences and applied at genome scale. The text-sequence model is directly tied to the biological discovery task.","evidence_snippet":"\"deep protein language models\" and a \"deep learning-based GR identification model trained on 74,872 curated GR sequences\"."}]}
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0496.prompt.txt:51:[{"record_id":"rec_003961","title":"Deep Research of Deep Research: From Transformer to Agent, From AI to AI for Science","abstract":"one single LLM that can handle different modalities in distinct ways. The exploration of  multimodal  Evo is a biological foundation model trained on 9 trillion DNA base pairs from a highly","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.28361","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003962","title":"AI and machine learning in nutrition: the promise, the challenge, and recommendations","abstract":", nutrition research increasingly relies on the integration of multimodal data sources, such  as genomics, metabolomics, transcriptomics, clinical biomarkers, wearable devices, dietary","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"The American Journal …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003963","title":"Phenotypic Bioactivity Prediction as Open-set Biological Assay Querying","abstract":"The traditional drug discovery pipeline is severely bottlenecked by the need to design and execute bespoke biological assays for every new target and compound—a process that is both time-consuming and prohibitively expensive. While machine learning has accelerated virtual screening, current models remain confined to “closed-set” paradigms, unable to generalize to entirely novel biological assays without target-specific experimental data. Here, we present\n                  OpenPheno\n                  , a groundbreaking multimodal foundation model that fundamentally redefines bioactivity prediction as an open-set, visual-language question-answering (QA) task. By integrating chemical structures (SMILES), universal phenotypic profiles (Cell Painting images), and natural language descriptions of biological assays, OpenPheno unlocks the highly coveted\n                  “profile once, predict many” paradigm\n                  . Instead of conducting countless target-specific wet-lab experiments, researchers only need to capture a single, low-cost Cell Painting image of a novel compound. OpenPheno then evaluates this universal phenotypic “fingerprint” against the text-based description of any unseen assay, predicting bioactivity in a zero-shot manner. On 54 entirely unseen assays, it achieves strong zero-shot performance (mean AUROC 0.75), exceeding supervised baselines trained with full labeled data, and few-shot adaptation further improves predictions. In the most stringent setting where both compounds and assays are novel, OpenPheno maintains robust generalization (mean AUROC 0.66), opening up a new paradigm for a highly scalable, cost-effective, and universal engine for next-generation drug discovery.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003964","title":"DISGENET: accelerating data-driven discovery in disease genomics and therapeutic development","abstract":"Abstract\n                \n                  Precision medicine and therapeutic development rely on a comprehensive understanding of genotype–phenotype relationships, yet this information remains fragmented across diverse sources. DISGENET, established over 15 years ago, addresses this challenge by systematically integrating gene–disease, variant–disease, and disease–disease associations from authoritative databases and the literature. This major upgrade expands coverage with chemical and pharmacological annotations and integrates biobank and clinical data. An advanced natural language processing (NLP) pipeline captures emerging evidence with full provenance and contextual details, key to streamlining data-driven insights. DISGENET supports diverse users through multiple tools, including an intuitive web interface, a REST API, an R package, a Cytoscape app, and an AI assistant for natural language queries. Quarterly updates ensure data currency, while a sustainable freemium model provides free academic access and supports ongoing development. DISGENET aims to accelerate data-driven discoveries and advance precision medicine and drug development. The platform is accessible at\n                  https://www.disgenet.com\n                  .\n                \n                \n                  Graphical Abstract\n                  \n                    \n                  \n                \n                \n                  Highlights\n                  \n                    \n                      DISGENET accelerates precision medicine via comprehensive genotype–phenotype data\n                    \n                    \n                      Access to gene, variant, disease, and drug data in a single platform\n                    \n                    \n                      Up-to-date evidence with provenance and full context\n                    \n                    \n                      Suite of tools to serve diverse research and clinical communities\n                    \n                    \n                      Sustainable freemium model ensures ongoing platform innovation.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003965","title":"Unifying the odyssey: artificial intelligence for rare disease diagnosis and therapy","abstract":"Genomic data is also sparse and less structured in essential content compared to human  language, so LLM  ; we envision that multimodal AI learners will fuse genomics, proteomics,","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Health and …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003966","title":"The Landscape of Computational Pathology Agents From Static Analysis to Autonomous Diagnostic Workflows","abstract":"to interrogate a slide, integrate multimodal clinical context, or  —such as H&E, and  transcriptomics. This fusion enables the agent  guided medical text foundation model. In","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Authorea Preprints","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003967","title":"MechPert: Mechanistic Consensus as an Inductive Bias for Unseen Perturbation Prediction","abstract":"We first characterized the limits of LLM reasoning within transcriptomic space. We found  that the raw predictions of efficient models, such as Gemini 3 Flash and GPT 5 mini, were","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"arXiv preprint arXiv …","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_003968","title":"De Novo Multi‐Mechanism Antimicrobial Peptide Design via Multimodal Deep Learning","abstract":"ABSTRACT\n                \n                  Artificial intelligence (AI)-driven discovery of antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) is yet to fully utilise their three-dimensional (3D) structural characteristics, microbial specie-specific antimicrobial activities and mechanisms. Here, we constructed a QLAPD database comprising the sequence, structures and antimicrobial properties of 12,914 AMPs. QLAPD underlies a multimodal, multitask, multilabel, and conditionally controlled AMP discovery (M3-CAD) pipeline, which is proposed for the\n                  de novo\n                  design of multi-mechanism AMPs to combat multidrug-resistant organisms (MDROs). This pipeline integrates the generation, regression, and classification modules, using a innovative 3D voxel coloring method to capture the nuanced physicochemical context of amino acids, significantly enhancing structural characterizations. QL-AMP-1, discovered by M3-CAD, which possesses four antimicrobial mechanisms, exhibited low toxicity and significant activity against MDROs. The skin wound infection model demonstrates its considerable antimicrobial effects and negligible toxicity. Altogether, integrating 3D features, specie-specific antimicrobial activities and mechanisms enhanced AI-driven AMP discovery, making the M3-CAD pipeline a viable tool for\n                  de novo\n                  AMP design.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"Advanced …","sources":["google_scholar"]}]
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0380.stderr.log:524:./data/model_catalog/all_models_catalog.csv:24:new_rec_000246,OmicsLM,,new_include_2026-06-10,topup_2026-04-15_to_2026-06-10,new_update_screening_include,rec_000246,OmicsLM: A Multimodal Large Language Model for Multi-Sample Omics Reasoning,2026,,,,arxiv; google_scholar,,,INCLUDE,python_gate,All first-pass criteria were positive without unresolved uncertainty.,,No OmicsLM weights/code found,arXiv: https://arxiv.org/html/2605.06728v1,"Paper lists baseline/resources like C2S, Qwen, BioMaze, PDEX, but no public OmicsLM checkpoint/repo found in text.",PDF downloaded,/Users/bogdan.didenko/lpnu/review/data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/full_texts/rec_000246_OmicsLM_A_Multimodal_Large_Language_Model_for_Multi-Sample,"Interpreting transcriptomic data is one of the most common analytical tasks in modern biology. Yet most current models either consume expression profiles without producing natural-language biological explanations, or reason in language without direct access to quantitative omics measurements. We introduce OmicsLM, a multimodal LLM that connects quantitative omics profiles with natural-language biological tasks. OmicsLM represents each transcriptomic profile as a compact continuous representation within the LLM context. This interface preserves quantitative expression signal while allowing natural-language instructions, explicit gene mentions, and multiple interleaved biological samples to be processed together in one model context. We train OmicsLM on more than 5.5 million instruction-following examples spanning over 70 task types, combining continuous transcriptomic inputs, experimental data rendered through diverse language templates, and free-text biological knowledge and question-answering data. This mixture covers cell type annotation, perturbation prediction, clinical prediction, pathway reasoning, and open-ended biological question answering. Existing benchmarks evaluate either profile-level prediction or text-only biological QA, leaving language-guided, multi-sample reasoning over real expression profiles unmeasured. To close this gap, we introduce GEO-OmicsQA, a benchmark for multi-sample biological question answering built from real Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) studies. We demonstrate that OmicsLM can use expression profiles directly and perform comparably to specialized omics models on profile-level tasks, while outperforming both omics-specialized models and general LLMs on language-guided biological reasoning over expression data."
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0380.stderr.log:1867:data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0379.stdout.log:1:{"results":[{"record_id":"rec_003025","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a knowledge hub and RAG-based assistant built on curated potato papers, not a new model. It uses LLMs to answer genomics queries from a curated database.","evidence_snippet":"\"leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs)\" and \"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture\" for the \"Potato Knowledge Hub\""},{"record_id":"rec_003026","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This proposes a new spatial transcriptomics interpolation model. The abstract describes ST slices, genes, and H&E images, but no substantive text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"we propose C2-STi\" for \"interpolating missing ST slices\" with a \"cross-modal alignment module\" using ST and H&E images"},{"record_id":"rec_003027","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This presents a new framework for scRNA-seq that converts gene descriptions into embeddings with LLMs. The text annotations are used directly as part of the model, creating a substantive text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"leverages gene-specific textual annotations\" and \"transform these descriptions into vector embedding representations using large language models (LLMs)\""},{"record_id":"rec_003028","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a benchmark for evaluating LLMs on single-cell tasks, not a primary model paper. It packages multi-omics QA tasks and reports model performance across LLMs.","evidence_snippet":"\"we introduce CellVerse, a unified language-centric question-answering benchmark\" and \"systematically evaluate\" 14 LLMs"},{"record_id":"rec_003029","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an LLM workflow for single-cell annotation using a graph-structured marker database, not a new foundational model. The LLM is wrapped around retrieval and annotation steps.","evidence_snippet":"\"domain-specific knowledge graph retrieval-augmented LLMs workflow for single-cell annotation\""},{"record_id":"rec_003030","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This proposes a new probabilistic CCA method for multi-modal data integration. The abstract is about bioinformatics and cancer data, but it does not describe any text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"multi-modal data integration\" and application to \"TCGA cancer datasets\" with \"no text component\" described"},{"record_id":"rec_003031","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a conversational bioinformatics platform that uses LLMs to generate and execute analysis code. It is an application framework rather than a new text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"platform that enables researchers to perform bioinformatics analyses using natural language\" and \"generates and executes bioinformatics code\""},{"record_id":"rec_003032","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is explicitly a survey of LLMs in bioinformatics, so it is not a primary model paper. It summarizes prior work across DNA, RNA, proteins, and single-cell data.","evidence_snippet":"\"Large Language Models in Bioinformatics: A Survey\" and \"This survey provides a systematic review of recent advancements\""}]}
